1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,256
Aarav cycles past a grey box on
an electric pole near his college.

2
00:00:05,256 --> 00:00:06,566
It hums softly.

3
00:00:06,916 --> 00:00:10,405
The wires going in carry
about eleven thousand volts.

4
00:00:10,405 --> 00:00:14,633
The wires going out to homes carry
about two hundred and thirty.

5
00:00:14,983 --> 00:00:18,500
How does a box with no moving
parts change the voltage?

6
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:20,974
That box is a transformer.

7
00:00:20,974 --> 00:00:24,680
Inside are two coils of
wire, wound on one iron core.

8
00:00:25,030 --> 00:00:28,080
The coil that takes in
the supply is the primary.

9
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:31,028
The coil that gives the
output is the secondary.

10
00:00:31,378 --> 00:00:33,225
The two coils never touch.

11
00:00:33,225 --> 00:00:35,531
They are linked only by magnetism.

12
00:00:35,531 --> 00:00:37,676
This is called mutual induction.

13
00:00:38,326 --> 00:00:40,313
Let's build one on the breadboard.

14
00:00:40,663 --> 00:00:45,218
An alternating current in the primary
makes a magnetic flux in the core.

15
00:00:45,218 --> 00:00:46,880
The flux keeps changing.

16
00:00:47,230 --> 00:00:51,274
That changing flux passes through
the secondary, and induces a voltage

17
00:00:51,274 --> 00:00:52,174
there.

18
00:00:52,197 --> 00:00:55,498
The output has the same
frequency as the input.

19
00:00:55,498 --> 00:00:57,170
Only its size changes.

20
00:00:57,820 --> 00:01:02,462
Here the primary has five hundred turns,
and the secondary has one hundred.

21
00:01:02,462 --> 00:01:05,886
Two hundred and thirty volts
in, forty six volts out.

22
00:01:06,236 --> 00:01:10,530
Now give the secondary one thousand
turns, more than the primary.

23
00:01:10,530 --> 00:01:13,797
The output rises to four
hundred and sixty volts.

24
00:01:14,147 --> 00:01:17,901
Fewer turns on the secondary
makes a step down transformer.

25
00:01:17,901 --> 00:01:20,489
More turns makes a step up transformer.

26
00:01:21,139 --> 00:01:22,434
Now try a battery.

27
00:01:22,434 --> 00:01:25,164
The flux builds up once, then stays still.

28
00:01:25,514 --> 00:01:27,697
A steady flux induces nothing.

29
00:01:27,697 --> 00:01:29,455
So the output is zero.

30
00:01:29,455 --> 00:01:32,724
A transformer works only
on alternating current.

31
00:01:33,374 --> 00:01:35,576
No change in flux, no output.

32
00:01:36,376 --> 00:01:38,332
Now the transformer equation.

33
00:01:38,682 --> 00:01:43,285
Every turn of wire, on either coil,
gets the same voltage from the changing

34
00:01:43,285 --> 00:01:44,185
flux.

35
00:01:44,517 --> 00:01:48,900
So the voltage across a coil is
proportional to its number of turns.

36
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:52,623
V s over V p equals N s over N p.

37
00:01:52,623 --> 00:01:55,174
This is called the turns ratio.

38
00:01:55,874 --> 00:01:59,291
In an ideal transformer,
no energy is lost.

39
00:01:59,291 --> 00:02:01,413
Power in equals power out.

40
00:02:01,413 --> 00:02:03,879
So V p I p equals V s I s.

41
00:02:04,579 --> 00:02:07,846
That means I s over I
p equals N p over N s.

42
00:02:07,846 --> 00:02:12,479
When the voltage steps up, the
current steps down by the same factor.

43
00:02:13,129 --> 00:02:14,245
Worked example.

44
00:02:14,245 --> 00:02:18,557
A transformer has two thousand turns
on the primary and one hundred on

45
00:02:18,557 --> 00:02:19,623
the secondary.

46
00:02:19,623 --> 00:02:22,441
The input is two hundred and thirty volts.

47
00:02:22,791 --> 00:02:27,071
The turns ratio is one hundred
over two thousand, one twentieth.

48
00:02:27,071 --> 00:02:30,578
So the output is two hundred
and thirty over twenty.

49
00:02:30,578 --> 00:02:32,296
Eleven point five volts.

50
00:02:32,896 --> 00:02:37,369
If the secondary gives two amperes,
the primary takes only one twentieth

51
00:02:37,369 --> 00:02:38,269
of that.

52
00:02:38,140 --> 00:02:39,781
Zero point one amperes.

53
00:02:40,431 --> 00:02:43,191
Real transformers do lose a little energy.

54
00:02:43,541 --> 00:02:45,851
Copper loss, as heat in the coils.

55
00:02:45,851 --> 00:02:49,544
Eddy current loss, from small
swirling currents in the core.

56
00:02:49,894 --> 00:02:54,598
That is why the core is made of thin
insulated sheets, called laminations.

57
00:02:54,948 --> 00:02:58,843
Hysteresis loss, as the core
is magnetised back and forth.

58
00:02:58,843 --> 00:03:02,474
And flux leakage, when some
flux misses the secondary.

59
00:03:02,824 --> 00:03:06,285
Efficiency is output power
divided by input power.

60
00:03:06,285 --> 00:03:11,001
Large transformers are very efficient,
but never one hundred percent.

61
00:03:11,651 --> 00:03:13,958
So why send power at high voltage?

62
00:03:13,958 --> 00:03:17,912
For the same power, a higher
voltage needs a smaller current.

63
00:03:18,262 --> 00:03:21,260
The heat lost in the wires is I squared R.

64
00:03:21,260 --> 00:03:25,020
Ten times less current means
one hundred times less loss.

65
00:03:25,670 --> 00:03:30,073
So power stations step the voltage
up, and transformers near our homes

66
00:03:30,073 --> 00:03:31,161
step it down.

67
00:03:31,161 --> 00:03:35,794
Transformers also work in power
supplies, welding machines and doorbells.

68
00:03:36,444 --> 00:03:37,908
Now, the exam trap.

69
00:03:38,258 --> 00:03:42,308
Many students think a step up
transformer also steps up the power.

70
00:03:42,658 --> 00:03:43,633
It does not.

71
00:03:43,633 --> 00:03:46,700
The voltage goes up, but
the current goes down.

72
00:03:46,700 --> 00:03:49,190
Power out is never more than power in.

73
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:51,446
Where is this asked?

74
00:03:51,446 --> 00:03:54,334
In PUC board papers, and in JEE Main.

75
00:03:54,334 --> 00:03:58,645
Use the turns ratio, find
currents, or find the efficiency.

76
00:03:59,295 --> 00:04:00,195
Try this.

77
00:04:00,118 --> 00:04:04,393
A transformer has four hundred primary
turns and two thousand secondary

78
00:04:04,393 --> 00:04:07,645
turns, on a two hundred
and thirty volt supply.

79
00:04:07,645 --> 00:04:09,752
It is ninety percent efficient.

80
00:04:09,752 --> 00:04:14,339
If the primary current is five
amperes, what is the secondary current?

81
00:04:19,739 --> 00:04:20,639
A.

82
00:04:20,233 --> 00:04:22,270
Zero point nine amperes.

83
00:04:22,620 --> 00:04:27,125
The turns ratio is five, so the
output is one thousand one hundred and

84
00:04:27,125 --> 00:04:28,098
fifty volts.

85
00:04:28,448 --> 00:04:32,862
Input power is two hundred and thirty
times five, which is one thousand

86
00:04:32,862 --> 00:04:34,783
one hundred and fifty watts.

87
00:04:34,783 --> 00:04:38,684
Ninety percent of that is one
thousand and thirty five watts.

88
00:04:39,034 --> 00:04:42,474
Divide by one thousand one
hundred and fifty volts.

89
00:04:42,474 --> 00:04:45,749
The secondary current is
zero point nine amperes.

90
00:04:46,399 --> 00:04:47,920
Screenshot this card.

91
00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:52,619
Next, a new chapter.

92
00:04:52,619 --> 00:04:56,227
Voltmeters and ammeters, for
direct and alternating current.

93
00:04:56,227 --> 00:04:59,384
How does a meter know how
much current is flowing?
